## 🛠️ Python & Git Command Cheat Sheet

### 1. Terminal & Navigation
* `python filename.py` — Executes a Python script.
* `dir` — Lists directory contents (PowerShell).
* `cd ..` — Moves up to the parent directory.

### 2. Git Workflow
* `git status` — Checks working tree status.
* `git remote -v` — Verifies remote GitHub repository URLs.
* `git add .` — Stages all changes.
* `git commit -m "message"` — Commits staged changes.
* `git push origin master` — Pushes commits to GitHub.

### 3. String & Data Parsing
* `string.lower()` — Converts text to lowercase.
* `string.strip()` — Removes leading/trailing whitespace.
* `string.startswith("val")` — Checks prefix matching.
* `string.split()` — Chops a string into pieces by spaces.
* `f"{value:.1f}"` — Formats numbers to 1 decimal place.

### 🔀 3. Git & GitHub Setup and Workflow

* **`git status`** — Checks the current state of your working tree (staged, modified, or clean).


* **`git remote -v`** — Verifies the connected remote GitHub repository URLs for fetching and pushing.


* **`git remote add origin <URL>`** — Links your local repository to your remote GitHub repository for the first time.


* **`git remote set-url origin <URL>`** — Updates or cleans up your remote repository URL.


* **`git add .`** — Stages all modified and new files in your project.
* **`git commit -m "Your message"`** — Records a snapshot of your staged changes with a descriptive commit message.
* **`git push origin master`** — Uploads your local commits to your remote GitHub repository on the `master` branch.

